{{--
  Applies a stored theme override before first paint, so a user who picked
  light or dark never sees a flash of the other one.

  Inline on purpose: an external script would be a network round-trip ahead of
  first paint, which is the exact flash this exists to prevent. Included
  (rather than copied) by partials/app-head.stx, partials/marketing-head.stx
  and layouts/auth.stx -- it was three hand-kept copies before migration
  Phase 5.

  layouts/status.stx deliberately does NOT use this: a status page owner can
  force light or dark for visitors, which the server stamps onto data-theme,
  so that page needs a resolver that leaves a forced value alone. See the
  script in that file.

  Nothing is written here; the toggle in partials/theme-toggle.stx is what
  stores the choice, under the same key.

  Strict mode flags the localStorage read and suggests @appearanceBootstrap.
  That directive does not fit: it reads a JSON preference object out of one
  key, so the bare 'light'/'dark' string written by theme-toggle.stx parses to
  nothing and every stored choice resolves to the default instead. It also
  requires a second 'appearance' axis this app does not have. The config-level
  colorMode boot script has the same storage shape as this code and would fit,
  but it is injected into every page -- including layouts/status.stx, where a
  page owner's forced theme has to win over a visitor's stored one, which
  neither mechanism can express. So this stays hand-written.
--}}
<script>
  (function () {
    try {
      // stx-strict-ignore-next-line window.localStorage
      var stored = localStorage.getItem('uptimestatus-theme')
      if (stored === 'light' || stored === 'dark') {
        document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', stored)
      }
    }
    catch (err) {}
  })()
</script>
